# Bird & Robinson - Modern Dental Assisting (Elsevier).
# Chapter list: 15th edition, from the publisher's table of contents (us.elsevierhealth.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "History of Dentistry", "Dentistry dates to ancient Egypt and China. Hippocrates and Aristotle wrote about teeth. Pierre Fauchard (1700s) is the father of modern dentistry. Horace Wells first used nitrous oxide as an anesthetic, G.V. Black standardized cavity preparation, and Wilhelm Roentgen discovered X-rays in 1895. C. Edmund Kells is credited with employing the first dental assistant. The first dental school was the Baltimore College of Dental Surgery (1840).", ["Fauchard = father of modern dentistry", "Horace Wells: nitrous oxide", "G.V. Black: cavity classification", "Roentgen discovered X-rays"]),
    ch(2, "The Dental Healthcare Team", "The team includes the dentist (DDS or DMD), registered dental hygienist (RDH), dental assistant, dental laboratory technician, and business staff. ADA-recognized specialties include endodontics, orthodontics, periodontics, prosthodontics, pediatric dentistry, oral and maxillofacial surgery, oral pathology, oral radiology, dental public health, dental anesthesiology, oral medicine, and orofacial pain.", ["DDS or DMD", "RDH = hygienist", "Lab technicians make prostheses", "ADA-recognized specialties"]),
    ch(3, "The Professional Dental Assistant", "A professional dental assistant is reliable, ethical, and well-groomed and keeps learning. The DANB (Dental Assisting National Board) gives the CDA credential; the ADAA (American Dental Assistants Association) is the professional association. Expanded functions are regulated by each state's dental practice act.", ["DANB gives CDA", "ADAA = professional association", "State dental practice acts", "Continuing education"]),
    ch(4, "Ethics and Code of Conduct in Dentistry", "Ethics are moral standards; the ADA and ADAA have codes of ethics. Principles include patient autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence, justice, and veracity. Ethical dilemmas are solved with a decision-making process. Confidentiality is essential.", ["Codes of ethics", "Autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence", "Confidentiality", "Ethical decision-making"]),
    ch(5, "Dentistry and the Law", "Each state's dental practice act defines legal duties and is enforced by the state board of dentistry. Assistants may work under direct supervision (dentist in the office and checks the work) or general supervision. Issues include patient abandonment, informed consent, malpractice, record keeping, and mandatory reporting of abuse. Respondeat superior makes dentists responsible for employees.", ["Dental practice act", "Direct vs. general supervision", "Informed consent", "Report suspected abuse"]),
    ch(6, "General Anatomy", "Anatomy uses planes (midsagittal, frontal, transverse) and directional terms. The body has cells, tissues (epithelial, connective, muscle, nerve), organs, and systems, plus the dorsal (cranial, spinal) and ventral (thoracic, abdominal, pelvic) cavities.", ["Midsagittal divides equal halves", "Four tissue types", "Dorsal and ventral cavities", "Directional terms"]),
    ch(7, "General Physiology", "The body's systems include skeletal, muscular, cardiovascular, lymphatic and immune, nervous, respiratory, digestive, endocrine, urinary, integumentary, and reproductive. Each works to maintain homeostasis.", ["Eleven body systems", "Homeostasis", "Systems interact", "Oral health affects general health"]),
    ch(8, "Oral Embryology and Histology", "Teeth develop in stages: bud, cap, bell, apposition, and maturation. Enamel (made by ameloblasts) is the hardest tissue in the body; dentin is made by odontoblasts; cementum covers the root. The pulp contains nerves and blood vessels. The periodontal ligament attaches the tooth to the alveolar bone. Cleft lip and palate form during embryonic development.", ["Enamel = hardest tissue", "Ameloblasts make enamel; odontoblasts make dentin", "Bud, cap, bell stages", "PDL attaches tooth to bone"]),
    ch(9, "Head and Neck Anatomy", "Key bones include the maxilla and mandible (the only movable skull bone). The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) allows hinge and gliding movements. Muscles of mastication are the masseter (strongest), temporalis, and medial and lateral pterygoids. The trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V) supplies the teeth. Salivary glands are the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual.", ["Mandible is the only movable skull bone", "Masseter is strongest chewing muscle", "Trigeminal nerve (CN V) supplies teeth", "Parotid is largest salivary gland"]),
    ch(10, "Landmarks of the Face and Oral Cavity", "Facial landmarks include the nasion, philtrum, ala of the nose, and vermilion border. Oral landmarks include the vestibule, frenum (labial and lingual), gingiva, hard and soft palate, uvula, incisive papilla, and tongue papillae.", ["Vermilion border of lips", "Frenum attachments", "Hard vs. soft palate", "Incisive papilla"]),
    ch(11, "Overview of the Dentitions", "Children have 20 primary teeth; adults have 32 permanent teeth. Mixed dentition (ages 6–12) has both. Numbering systems: Universal (permanent 1–32, primary A–T), Palmer, and FDI (two digits). Tooth surfaces include mesial, distal, facial (buccal or labial), lingual, occlusal, and incisal. Angle's classification describes occlusion.", ["20 primary, 32 permanent", "Universal: 1–32 and A–T", "Mesial toward midline", "Angle's Class I, II, III"]),
    ch(12, "Tooth Morphology", "Incisors cut, canines tear (longest root: maxillary canine), premolars grind and tear, and molars grind. The maxillary first molar has three roots; mandibular molars usually have two. Features include cusps, ridges, fossae, and grooves.", ["Incisors cut, molars grind", "Maxillary canine longest root", "Maxillary molars: 3 roots", "Mandibular molars: 2 roots"]),
    ch(13, "Preventive Dentistry", "Prevention includes brushing twice daily with fluoride toothpaste, flossing, sealants, fluoride (systemic and topical; community water fluoridation), diet counseling, and regular checkups. Plaque (biofilm) causes caries and gum disease. Brushing methods include the modified Bass technique.", ["Fluoride strengthens enamel", "Plaque = biofilm", "Modified Bass brushing", "Sealants protect pits and fissures"]),
    ch(14, "Nutrition", "Good nutrition supports oral health. Fermentable carbohydrates (sugars) feed caries-causing bacteria; frequency of sugar intake matters more than amount. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy (bleeding gums); calcium and vitamin D build teeth and bones. Eating disorders like bulimia erode enamel.", ["Sugar frequency drives caries", "Vitamin C deficiency → bleeding gums", "Calcium and vitamin D", "Bulimia erodes enamel"]),
    ch(15, "Dental Caries", "Caries (tooth decay) is caused by bacteria—mainly Streptococcus mutans and Lactobacillus—that produce acids from sugars, demineralizing enamel. Early lesions (white spots) can remineralize with fluoride. Saliva protects teeth. Caries risk assessment and detection tools guide prevention.", ["S. mutans causes caries", "Acid demineralizes enamel", "White spot lesions can remineralize", "Saliva protects"]),
    ch(16, "Periodontal Disease", "Gingivitis is reversible gum inflammation caused by plaque. Periodontitis destroys the supporting bone and ligament, causing pockets deeper than 3 mm and tooth loss. Risk factors include smoking, diabetes, and pregnancy. Treatment includes scaling and root planing.", ["Gingivitis is reversible", "Periodontitis destroys bone", "Pockets > 3 mm", "Smoking and diabetes increase risk"]),
    ch(17, "Oral Pathology", "Oral lesions include ulcers (aphthous ulcers/canker sores), herpes labialis (cold sores), candidiasis (thrush), leukoplakia (white patch, possibly precancerous), and oral cancer (often squamous cell carcinoma, linked to tobacco and alcohol). Other conditions include ankyloglossia, torus palatinus, and HIV-related lesions.", ["Leukoplakia may be precancerous", "Candidiasis = fungal", "Herpes labialis = cold sores", "Tobacco and alcohol → oral cancer"]),
    ch(18, "Microbiology", "Microorganisms include bacteria (cocci, bacilli, spirilla), viruses, fungi, and prions. Spores are the most resistant form of life. Diseases of concern include hepatitis B (vaccine available), hepatitis C, HIV, tuberculosis, and herpes.", ["Bacteria shapes", "Spores most resistant", "Hepatitis B vaccine", "Viruses need host cells"]),
    ch(19, "Disease Transmission and Infection Prevention", "The chain of infection requires an agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, and susceptible host. Standard precautions treat all patients as potentially infectious. PPE includes gloves, masks, eyewear, and gowns. Hand hygiene is the most important measure. OSHA's Bloodborne Pathogens Standard requires the hepatitis B vaccine to be offered.", ["Chain of infection", "Standard precautions", "Hand hygiene most important", "OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard"]),
    ch(20, "Principles and Techniques of Disinfection", "Clinical surfaces are protected with barriers or cleaned and disinfected with EPA-registered intermediate-level (tuberculocidal) disinfectants. Spaulding classifies items as critical (penetrate tissue; sterilize), semicritical (touch mucous membranes; sterilize or high-level disinfect), and noncritical (touch intact skin; disinfect).", ["Critical, semicritical, noncritical", "EPA-registered disinfectants", "Surface barriers", "Pre-clean before disinfecting"]),
    ch(21, "Principles and Techniques of Instrument Processing and Sterilization", "Instrument processing: transport, cleaning (ultrasonic cleaner), packaging, sterilization, storage. Sterilization methods include steam autoclave (most common; about 250°F/121°C), chemical vapor, and dry heat. Biological monitoring (spore tests) weekly confirms sterilization; chemical indicators show exposure only.", ["Autoclave = steam under pressure", "Spore testing weekly", "Ultrasonic cleaning", "Chemical indicators ≠ proof of sterility"]),
    ch(22, "Regulatory and Advisory Agencies", "The CDC makes recommendations; OSHA enforces worker safety; the EPA regulates disinfectants and waste; the FDA regulates medical devices and drugs. OSHA requires an exposure control plan and Hazard Communication Standard compliance.", ["CDC recommends", "OSHA protects workers", "EPA regulates disinfectants", "FDA regulates devices"]),
    ch(23, "Chemical and Waste Management", "OSHA's Hazard Communication Standard requires labels, Safety Data Sheets (SDS), and training. Waste types include general waste, contaminated waste, regulated (biohazard) waste such as sharps and blood-soaked items, and hazardous waste like amalgam scrap (use amalgam separators).", ["Safety Data Sheets", "Sharps in puncture-proof containers", "Biohazard labeling", "Amalgam waste recycled"]),
    ch(24, "Dental Unit Water Quality", "Dental unit waterlines grow biofilm. The CDC recommends treatment water meet drinking water standards (500 CFU/mL or less). Methods include independent reservoirs, chemical treatment, flushing lines, and using sterile water for surgery.", ["Biofilm in waterlines", "≤ 500 CFU/mL", "Flush waterlines", "Sterile water for surgery"]),
    ch(25, "Occupational Hazards", "Hazards include sharps injuries, chemicals, radiation, noise, latex allergy, and musculoskeletal disorders. Ergonomics—neutral position, proper seating—prevents strain. Report needlestick injuries immediately.", ["Report sharps injuries", "Ergonomics prevents strain", "Latex allergy", "Neutral working position"]),
    ch(26, "The Patient's Dental Record", "The dental record is a legal document including registration, medical and dental history, informed consent, charting, and treatment notes. Charting uses symbols and colors (often red for treatment needed and blue for completed work). Errors are corrected with a single line, initials, and date.", ["Record is legal document", "Update medical history every visit", "Single-line corrections", "Charting symbols"]),
    ch(27, "Vital Signs", "Vital signs include temperature (normal about 98.6°F), pulse (60–100), respiration (12–20), and blood pressure (normal under 120/80). Measure before procedures, especially anesthesia. The radial pulse is most common for adults.", ["Temp ~98.6°F", "Pulse 60–100", "BP < 120/80 normal", "Radial pulse"]),
    ch(28, "Oral Diagnosis and Treatment Planning", "The dentist examines extraoral and intraoral structures, teeth, and periodontium, using radiographs and other tests. Classification of cavities (G.V. Black Classes I–VI) and the periodontal charting guide treatment. The treatment plan is presented to the patient for consent.", ["G.V. Black Classes I–VI", "Extraoral and intraoral exam", "Periodontal charting", "Treatment plan consent"]),
    ch(29, "The Special Needs and Medically Compromised Patient", "Special patients include older adults, people with disabilities, pregnant patients (second trimester is best for treatment), and patients with heart conditions, diabetes, bleeding disorders, or cancer. Some heart conditions need antibiotic premedication.", ["Second trimester for pregnant patients", "Antibiotic premedication", "Diabetes affects healing", "Wheelchair transfers"]),
    ch(30, "Principles of Pharmacology", "Drugs are prescribed using parts of a prescription (superscription, inscription, subscription, signature). Controlled substances (Schedules I–V) are regulated by the DEA. Common dental drugs include antibiotics, analgesics, and anesthetics. Patients' medications must be reviewed for interactions.", ["Prescription parts", "DEA schedules I–V", "Analgesics and antibiotics", "Review medications"]),
    ch(31, "Assisting in a Medical Emergency", "Prevention through medical histories is key. Emergencies include syncope (most common; place in subsupine position), angina (nitroglycerin), myocardial infarction, asthma, allergic reactions (epinephrine for anaphylaxis), hypoglycemia (give sugar), seizures, and airway obstruction. The emergency kit includes oxygen and an AED.", ["Syncope most common", "Subsupine position for fainting", "Epinephrine for anaphylaxis", "Sugar for hypoglycemia"]),
    ch(32, "The Dental Office", "The office includes the reception area, treatment rooms, sterilization area, lab, and darkroom or imaging area. The dental chair, operator stools, lights, and units are maintained daily. Opening and closing routines keep the office safe.", ["Treatment room setup", "Sterilization area", "Daily equipment care", "Opening and closing routines"]),
    ch(33, "Delivering Dental Care", "Four-handed dentistry uses an operator and assistant working efficiently. Operating zones are described as clock positions (right-handed operator: 7–12 o'clock; assistant: 2–4 o'clock; transfer zone 4–7). Instruments are transferred in the transfer zone using a pen grasp and parallel position.", ["Four-handed dentistry", "Assistant zone 2–4 o'clock", "Transfer zone 4–7 o'clock", "Patient supine"]),
    ch(34, "Dental Hand Instruments", "Instruments have a handle, shank, and working end. Examination instruments include the mouth mirror, explorer, and periodontal probe. Others include hand cutting instruments (excavators, hoes, chisels), restorative instruments (amalgam carrier, condensers, carvers, burnishers), and cotton pliers. Instruments are arranged in order of use.", ["Mirror, explorer, cotton pliers", "Periodontal probe measures pockets", "Spoon excavator removes decay", "Arrange in order of use"]),
    ch(35, "Dental Handpieces and Accessories", "Handpieces include high-speed (for cutting tooth structure; uses water spray) and low-speed (for polishing, finishing, and caries removal). Burs come in shapes such as round, inverted cone, pear, and fissure. Handpieces must be sterilized after each patient.", ["High-speed cuts enamel", "Low-speed polishes", "Bur shapes", "Sterilize handpieces"]),
    ch(36, "Moisture Control", "Moisture control keeps the field dry. Tools include the high-volume evacuator (HVE), saliva ejector, air-water syringe, cotton rolls, and the dental dam (rubber dam), which isolates teeth and protects the patient.", ["HVE removes fluid and debris", "Saliva ejector", "Dental dam isolates teeth", "Cotton rolls"]),
    ch(37, "Anesthesia and Pain Control", "Topical anesthetic numbs the surface. Local anesthetics (lidocaine most common) block nerve impulses; vasoconstrictors (epinephrine) prolong effect and reduce bleeding. Injection types include the inferior alveolar nerve block (mandibular) and infiltration (maxillary). Nitrous oxide-oxygen sedation reduces anxiety.", ["Lidocaine most common", "Epinephrine prolongs anesthesia", "Inferior alveolar block", "Nitrous oxide sedation"]),
    ch(38, "Foundation of Dental Radiology", "X-rays are ionizing radiation produced in the tube head when electrons hit the tungsten target. Kilovoltage (kVp) controls quality (penetration and contrast); milliamperage (mA) and time control quantity (density). Radiolucent areas look dark; radiopaque areas look light.", ["kVp controls penetration", "mA controls quantity", "Radiolucent = dark", "Radiopaque = light"]),
    ch(39, "Dental Imaging: Techniques and Processing", "Images can be digital (sensors or phosphor plates) or film. Digital imaging uses less radiation and allows instant viewing. Common errors include elongation, foreshortening, overlapping, and cone cutting.", ["Digital uses less radiation", "Elongation and foreshortening", "Cone cut", "Overlapping"]),
    ch(40, "Compliance and Prevention in Dental Radiography", "Radiation safety follows ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable). Protect patients with lead or lead-equivalent aprons and thyroid collars, fast image receptors, and proper technique. Operators stand at least 6 feet away and wear dosimeters.", ["ALARA", "Thyroid collar", "Stand 6 feet away", "Dosimeter badges"]),
    ch(41, "Intraoral Imaging", "Intraoral images include periapical (whole tooth and root), bitewing (crowns of upper and lower teeth; detect interproximal caries), and occlusal. Techniques are paralleling (preferred) and bisecting the angle. A full-mouth series combines periapicals and bitewings.", ["Bitewings detect interproximal decay", "Periapical shows root tip", "Paralleling preferred", "Full-mouth series"]),
    ch(42, "Extraoral Imaging", "Extraoral images include panoramic (whole jaws on one image), cephalometric (for orthodontics), and cone beam computed tomography (CBCT, 3D). Patient positioning errors in panoramics distort images.", ["Panoramic shows both jaws", "Cephalometric for ortho", "CBCT = 3D", "Positioning matters"]),
    ch(43, "Restorative and Esthetic Dental Materials", "Restorative materials include amalgam (silver, tin, copper, mercury; strong for posterior teeth), composite resin (tooth-colored, light-cured), glass ionomer (releases fluoride), and ceramics. Properties include strength, thermal conductivity, and esthetics.", ["Amalgam contains mercury", "Composite is tooth-colored", "Glass ionomer releases fluoride", "Light-cured materials"]),
    ch(44, "Dental Liners, Bases, and Bonding Systems", "Liners (calcium hydroxide) protect the pulp; varnishes seal tubules; bases provide thermal insulation. Bonding systems use etchant (phosphoric acid), primer, and adhesive to bond composites to tooth structure.", ["Calcium hydroxide protects pulp", "Bases insulate", "Etch, prime, bond", "Phosphoric acid etchant"]),
    ch(45, "Dental Cements", "Cements are used for luting (permanent cementation of crowns), temporary cementation, and bases. Types include zinc phosphate (exothermic; mix on a cool glass slab), zinc oxide-eugenol (soothing; temporary), glass ionomer, polycarboxylate, and resin cements.", ["Zinc phosphate: cool slab", "ZOE soothes pulp", "Glass ionomer releases fluoride", "Luting = permanent cementation"]),
    ch(46, "Impression Materials and Techniques", "Impressions record teeth and tissues. Alginate (irreversible hydrocolloid) is used for preliminary impressions and study models; it must be poured promptly. Elastomeric materials (polyvinyl siloxane, polyether) are used for final impressions. Bite registrations record occlusion.", ["Alginate for study models", "Pour alginate promptly", "PVS for final impressions", "Bite registration"]),
    ch(47, "Laboratory Materials and Procedures", "Lab materials include gypsum products (plaster for study models; stone and die stone for working casts), waxes, and acrylic resins. Procedures include pouring and trimming models and making custom trays and bleaching trays.", ["Plaster for study models", "Die stone strongest", "Custom trays", "Trim models"]),
    ch(48, "General Dentistry", "General dentistry restores teeth with amalgam and composite restorations, classified by G.V. Black. Procedures follow steps: anesthesia, isolation, cavity preparation, liners/bases, restoration placement, and finishing. Veneers and bleaching are esthetic procedures.", ["Class I = pits and fissures", "Class II = posterior proximal", "Restoration steps", "Veneers and bleaching"]),
    ch(49, "Matrix Systems for Restorative Dentistry", "A matrix replaces a missing wall of a tooth during restoration. The Tofflemire retainer and band are used for amalgam in posterior teeth; clear strips are used for anterior composites. Wedges hold the band against the tooth and prevent overhangs.", ["Tofflemire for Class II amalgam", "Clear strips for composites", "Wedges prevent overhangs", "Band replaces missing wall"]),
    ch(50, "Fixed Prosthodontics", "Fixed prostheses include crowns (full or partial coverage), bridges (pontic plus abutments), inlays, onlays, and veneers. Steps include preparation, gingival retraction cord, final impression, provisional, and cementation. CAD/CAM can make same-day restorations.", ["Bridge: pontic + abutments", "Retraction cord", "CAD/CAM", "Crowns cover teeth"]),
    ch(51, "Provisional Coverage", "Provisional (temporary) crowns and bridges protect prepared teeth, maintain position, and restore function while permanent restorations are made. They're made from preformed crowns or custom acrylic/composite and cemented with temporary cement.", ["Protects prepared tooth", "Custom or preformed", "Temporary cement", "Maintains spacing"]),
    ch(52, "Removable Prosthodontics", "Removable prostheses include partial dentures (with clasps and rests) and complete dentures. Steps include impressions, bite registration, try-in, and delivery. Patients are taught care and cleaning. Immediate dentures are placed right after extractions.", ["Partial and complete dentures", "Clasps and rests", "Immediate dentures", "Denture care"]),
    ch(53, "Dental Implants", "Implants are titanium posts placed in the jawbone that fuse with bone (osseointegration). Candidates need adequate bone and good health. Implants support crowns, bridges, or dentures and need good home care.", ["Titanium posts", "Osseointegration", "Need adequate bone", "Support crowns/dentures"]),
    ch(54, "Endodontics", "Endodontics treats the pulp. Pulpitis can be reversible or irreversible. Diagnostic tests include percussion, palpation, thermal tests, and electric pulp tests. Root canal therapy removes the pulp, shapes and cleans canals (files, irrigation with sodium hypochlorite), and fills them with gutta-percha.", ["Root canal removes pulp", "Gutta-percha fills canals", "Sodium hypochlorite irrigant", "Pulp vitality tests"]),
    ch(55, "Periodontics", "Periodontics treats gum and bone disease. Procedures include periodontal charting (probing), scaling and root planing, gingivectomy, flap surgery, and grafts. Periodontal maintenance visits are often every 3 months.", ["Probing depths", "Scaling and root planing", "Gingivectomy", "3-month maintenance"]),
    ch(56, "Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery", "Oral surgery includes simple and surgical extractions, impacted third molars, biopsies, and implant placement, using sterile technique. Instruments include forceps, elevators, and rongeurs. Post-op instructions cover bleeding (bite on gauze) and avoiding straws to prevent dry socket (alveolar osteitis).", ["Forceps and elevators", "Sterile technique", "Avoid straws: dry socket", "Bite on gauze"]),
    ch(57, "Pediatric Dentistry", "Pediatric dentistry cares for children through adolescence. Behavior management includes tell-show-do. Procedures include sealants, pulpotomy, and stainless steel crowns. Early childhood caries ('baby bottle tooth decay') is prevented by not putting children to bed with bottles. Assistants must report suspected child abuse.", ["Tell-show-do", "Stainless steel crowns", "Pulpotomy", "Early childhood caries"]),
    ch(58, "Coronal Polishing", "Coronal polishing removes plaque and extrinsic stains from crowns of teeth using a prophy angle and abrasive paste. It's cosmetic, not a substitute for scaling. Use a light, intermittent stroke and a fulcrum. Intrinsic stains can't be polished off.", ["Removes extrinsic stains", "Light intermittent pressure", "Use a fulcrum", "Intrinsic stains don't polish off"]),
    ch(59, "Dental Sealants", "Sealants are resin coatings placed in pits and fissures of molars and premolars to prevent caries. Steps: clean, isolate, etch, rinse, dry (chalky appearance), apply, cure, check occlusion. Moisture contamination is the main cause of failure.", ["Prevent pit and fissure caries", "Etch → chalky appearance", "Moisture causes failure", "Check occlusion"]),
    ch(60, "Orthodontics", "Orthodontics corrects malocclusion (Angle's Class I, II, III). Appliances include fixed braces (brackets, bands, archwires, ligatures) and removable appliances (clear aligners, retainers). Separators open space before banding. Retainers maintain results.", ["Angle's classes", "Brackets and archwires", "Separators before bands", "Retainers maintain results"]),
    ch(61, "Communication in the Dental Office", "Good communication includes verbal and nonverbal skills, active listening, and professional phone etiquette. Handling difficult patients calmly and respecting cultural differences builds trust. Written communication must be clear and professional.", ["Active listening", "Phone etiquette", "Cultural sensitivity", "Professional written communication"]),
    ch(62, "Business Operating Systems", "Business tasks include appointment scheduling (buffer time), recall systems, inventory management (reorder point), and records management. Practice management software handles much of this. HIPAA protects patient information.", ["Scheduling and recall", "Inventory reorder point", "Practice management software", "HIPAA"]),
    ch(63, "Financial Management in the Dental Office", "Financial tasks include fee schedules, accounts receivable, insurance claims (CDT codes for dental procedures), collections, and payroll. Dental insurance terms include deductibles, copayments, and preauthorization.", ["CDT procedure codes", "Accounts receivable", "Insurance claims", "Deductibles and copays"]),
    ch(64, "Marketing Your Skills", "Job searches include setting goals, writing résumés and cover letters, interviewing, and sending thank-you notes. Professionalism, credentials (CDA), and continuing education help career growth.", ["Résumé and cover letter", "Interview preparation", "Thank-you notes", "Credentials help careers"]),
]

